\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Measured cost of one release round of the server-side protocol in semi-honest two-party computation (CrypTen, two processes on one machine, local network; median of 3 repetitions). Bytes are those sent by one server as reported by CrypTen; rounds are communication rounds. Update channel: norm checks and masked sum of $K$ updates of dimension $d$. Gap channel: range checks, sort, trimmed mean and noise addition. Err: largest absolute fixed-point error of the trimmed mean (noise switched off), and of the update sum in the update block. The gap channel uses the validity bits of the update channel (a rejected update also removes the client's gap), so the full release round is the sum of the two blocks. Client-to-server input sharing and Beaver-triple generation are not included.}}{29}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:mpc}{{10}{29}{Measured cost of one release round of the server-side protocol in semi-honest two-party computation (CrypTen, two processes on one machine, local network; median of 3 repetitions). Bytes are those sent by one server as reported by CrypTen; rounds are communication rounds. Update channel: norm checks and masked sum of $K$ updates of dimension $d$. Gap channel: range checks, sort, trimmed mean and noise addition. Err: largest absolute fixed-point error of the trimmed mean (noise switched off), and of the update sum in the update block. The gap channel uses the validity bits of the update channel (a rejected update also removes the client's gap), so the full release round is the sum of the two blocks. Client-to-server input sharing and Beaver-triple generation are not included}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Balanced accuracy of the optimal test that distinguishes two attribute-neighbouring datasets from the released gap (closed form, worst case $|\Delta |=\Delta _{\mathrm  {TM}}$; $R=10$ releases, $\delta =10^{-5}$). $z=\sigma _d/\Delta _{\mathrm  {TM}}$ from the RDP calibration. Both servers honest: one release with variance $2\sigma _d^2$. One server colluding: it removes its own noise. All releases: the colluding server combines all $R$ releases of a target whose contribution is unchanged. Bound: $(e^{\varepsilon }+\delta )/(1+e^{\varepsilon })$.}}{30}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:attack}{{11}{30}{Balanced accuracy of the optimal test that distinguishes two attribute-neighbouring datasets from the released gap (closed form, worst case $|\Delta |=\Delta _{\mathrm {TM}}$; $R=10$ releases, $\delta =10^{-5}$). $z=\sigma _d/\Delta _{\mathrm {TM}}$ from the RDP calibration. Both servers honest: one release with variance $2\sigma _d^2$. One server colluding: it removes its own noise. All releases: the colluding server combines all $R$ releases of a target whose contribution is unchanged. Bound: $(e^{\varepsilon }+\delta )/(1+e^{\varepsilon })$}{table.11}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Privacy budget $\varepsilon $ of the gap channel and release period $P$ ($K=5$, 3 seeds): test DPD, mean$\pm $sd. Row $\infty $: no noise on the gap ($\sigma _d=0$, where the bias correction is the identity); these runs were made with $P\in \{1,10\}$ only. All other experiments use $\varepsilon =8$ and $P=10$.}}{30}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e5}{{12}{30}{Privacy budget $\varepsilon $ of the gap channel and release period $P$ ($K=5$, 3 seeds): test DPD, mean$\pm $sd. Row $\infty $: no noise on the gap ($\sigma _d=0$, where the bias correction is the identity); these runs were made with $P\in \{1,10\}$ only. All other experiments use $\varepsilon =8$ and $P=10$}{table.12}{}}
